WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
These methane-making microbes look like typical bacteria.

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.740
Why would a molecule place them on a different branch?

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.380
Under the microscope, both are tiny cells without nuclei.

4
00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.520
Shape gives us evidence, but it may hide the ancestry we want to test.

5
00:00:20.983 --> 00:00:26.083
To compare very different cells, scientists need the same kind of record from every

6
00:00:26.083 --> 00:00:30.143
sample. Every cell builds proteins on ribosomes.

7
00:00:31.543 --> 00:00:34.423
Ribosomal RNA forms much of each ribosome.

8
00:00:35.743 --> 00:00:40.103
Conserved parts let researchers line up distant samples, while differing parts

9
00:00:40.103 --> 00:00:41.103
provide evidence.

10
00:00:42.083 --> 00:00:46.623
Woese and Fox compared catalogues of short ribosomal RNA pieces.

11
00:00:48.123 --> 00:00:52.223
Picture each catalogue as a row of labelled tiles, not a complete molecule.

12
00:00:53.723 --> 00:00:55.403
Matching tiles pull the rows together.

13
00:00:56.803 --> 00:00:59.043
Fewer matches leave the rows farther apart.

14
00:01:00.243 --> 00:01:02.363
The pattern across pairs tests the tree.

15
00:01:03.417 --> 00:01:04.697
Here are hidden pairs.

16
00:01:06.037 --> 00:01:10.317
A pair shares many ribosomal RNA pieces, while another shares few.

17
00:01:11.537 --> 00:01:14.077
Which pair should sit closer on a tree of ancestry?

18
00:01:15.557 --> 00:01:17.777
Hold your answer before the labels appear.

19
00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:28.923
The pair with many shared pieces belongs closer, if the rest of the evidence agrees.

20
00:01:30.303 --> 00:01:35.043
The methane makers matched each other far better than they matched typical bacteria.

21
00:01:36.463 --> 00:01:41.003
Typical bacteria also matched each other better than they matched methane makers.

22
00:01:42.283 --> 00:01:45.703
That repeated pattern challenged a combined prokaryote group.

23
00:01:46.717 --> 00:01:50.257
A classification is a testable map of relationships.

24
00:01:51.637 --> 00:01:55.437
A branch claims that its members share a more recent common ancestor.

25
00:01:56.757 --> 00:02:01.797
When several comparisons challenge that claim, scientists redraw only the supported

26
00:02:01.797 --> 00:02:05.757
part. The organisms themselves remain unchanged.

27
00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:11.310
The researchers interpreted this pattern as three major lines of descent.

28
00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:16.710
Later, the rank called domain named Bacteria, Archaea and Eukarya.

29
00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:20.030
The methane makers joined Archaea.

30
00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:22.310
Their cells did not change.

31
00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:25.850
The evidence changed the best-supported tree.

32
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:31.500
Molecular evidence does not automatically outrank every other clue.

33
00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:37.000
It adds a comparison that can expose misleading similarities in shape.

34
00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:42.260
Strong classifications weigh several markers and kinds of evidence.

35
00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.000
Agreement strengthens a branch.

36
00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:48.120
Conflict shows where to test next.

37
00:02:49.083 --> 00:02:52.263
The overlap scores are not percentages of DNA.

38
00:02:53.603 --> 00:02:55.343
They do not tell when branches split.

39
00:02:56.603 --> 00:03:01.023
They summarize shared pieces in these ribosomal RNA catalogues.

40
00:03:01.943 --> 00:03:08.483
A tree remains a claim tested against more organisms, other genes, membranes and any

41
00:03:08.483 --> 00:03:09.983
evidence that can challenge it.

42
00:03:10.967 --> 00:03:15.307
That is why changing a group after careful testing can be good science.

43
00:03:17.067 --> 00:03:21.207
Classification is a model of ancestry, not a permanent filing cabinet.

44
00:03:22.547 --> 00:03:27.367
When stronger evidence changes the picture, scientists revise the model while

45
00:03:27.367 --> 00:03:28.627
keeping its limits visible.
